#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/id #uuid "af294c61-bf5b-5b8a-a104-853ea1418cb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14586" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $76 \\cdot 122 \\textrm{ mod } 6.$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}76 & ≡12\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡12\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 122 & ≡20\\cdot 6+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡20\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}76\\cdot 122 & ≡4\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡6+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n76 \\cdot 122 \\equiv 2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151841 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "043fcc75-20fe-5027-8585-ab24d45ac89d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14587" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $5^{7} \\textrm{ mod } 8.$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n5^{7} = 5^2\\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 5^3.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 8+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡5\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{7} & ≡{5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5^{7} \\textrm{ mod } 8= 5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151856 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/id #uuid "aa58ae42-bdf6-5303-9c9c-2e20151f2ed8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14610" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $3x \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)?$ \n\n- $x  \\equiv 5 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $x  \\equiv 7 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $x  \\equiv 9 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ we have the following:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $3,$ and $7\\cdot 3 = 21$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 7\\cdot 3x & ≡7\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 21x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+20x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+0x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3x \\equiv 1  \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \nx \\equiv 7\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/id #uuid "21d3a5de-63f0-5150-9cb8-c2cbc609bb8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14651" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $17^{13}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a\\equiv b\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c\\equiv d\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac\\equiv bd  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a^k \\equiv b^k  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17 & ≡10+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{2} & ≡{7}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡49\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {17}^{4} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose $17^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n17^{13} =  \\left(17^4\\right)^3\\cdot 17\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{13} & ≡{({17}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 17\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{3}\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10 = 7\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of $17^{13}$ is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4533cdc0-e7c1-5be4-af99-d14164aad6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413485} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad09c7f-bf5b-5964-a4bd-7f93ec6f982b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413497} {:answer/id #uuid "94c1f521-2462-555d-bb1c-e0300f04a33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413511} {:answer/id #uuid "a82628f8-d912-5d63-838c-341eee3cbf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413515} {:answer/id #uuid "c34119e2-8819-5f06-b629-ec7caa33140b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c34119e2-8819-5f06-b629-ec7caa33140b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413524} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba6fab1-e43b-50d0-9c08-30ffdf3512e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82ba7d3c-5902-5665-8148-515fc5c0a008" :question/math-academy-id "q-211996" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $3x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)?$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $14$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $3,$ and $5\\cdot 3=15$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3x & ≡5\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ 15x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ x+14x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ x+0x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ x & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf00a28-bbe1-5808-ba3d-02015e277bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caf00a28-bbe1-5808-ba3d-02015e277bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413526} :answer-field/id #uuid "dea5876a-7981-5a10-9558-42412b898766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b81b6d2b-abd2-5154-9eb2-356526df808d" :question/math-academy-id "q-213022" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$84\\cdot 102\\text{ mod }5=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84 & ≡16\\cdot 5+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡16\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 102 & ≡20\\cdot 5+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡20\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84\\cdot 102 & ≡4\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡5+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n84\\cdot 102\\text{ mod }5=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007485a3-ad3a-5793-ac33-de29999bf9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413465} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff97b5a-aa26-5386-8610-53fd3f7e47a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413489} {:answer/id #uuid "51c72491-04d8-53e7-bd93-4d1d93e3e157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413490} {:answer/id #uuid "72adad8b-3fc8-5add-a319-a9ec180a9b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413499} {:answer/id #uuid "d45499bb-6b7d-582e-97b1-44d94a4e45b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ff97b5a-aa26-5386-8610-53fd3f7e47a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413489} :answer-field/id #uuid "73c85ead-eecb-5b52-b908-fbc0204c2c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc39a9be-d29f-5f9f-ba0f-0e6107657cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-213048" :question/problem "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${6}^{6}≡$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{6}^{6}={6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 8+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{6} & ≡{6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 8+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{6}^{6}≡0\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445ca29d-db97-5887-873d-532ed5265d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413484} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1fb128-4b44-5c1c-9611-722a75357454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413503} {:answer/id #uuid "92362405-fdac-5084-b46a-cad443aaa4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413510} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d3d4e7-0921-556d-a045-1ebf50a22c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413521} {:answer/id #uuid "eade3540-01db-5f38-a511-c8fde2263499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "445ca29d-db97-5887-873d-532ed5265d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413484} :answer-field/id #uuid "0588c800-d87c-56d9-83a6-8993a8964638" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8e0aa45-c7f0-5602-aeab-6e88c994c623" :question/math-academy-id "q-213055" :question/problem "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${20}^{4}≡$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}=(4\\cdot 5)^{4}={4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ {5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 6+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{20}^{4} & ≡{4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}≡4\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowle