\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{12}^{6}=(3\\cdot 4)^{6}={3}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3} & ≡27\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 9+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 9+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{6} & ≡{3}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot 0\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{6}≡0\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4fd226-e1ee-532e-9da7-b3a38912b44c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413474} {:answer/id #uuid "311985a2-423d-589b-bb01-4905e58b17b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413476} {:answer/id #uuid "72dfd0de-01f9-5745-9e80-7951963796da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413501} {:answer/id #uuid "745ac539-6595-5789-9996-9d6eca918d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)"