≡9\\cdot 7+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}≡1\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e13f034b-3533-5ff3-af42-5647e7b22ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e13f034b-3533-5ff3-af42-5647e7b22ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413532} :answer-field/id #uuid "b582a022-b816-570a-9ea2-aa5288c6cd78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33e605f0-847a-5675-b130-592bda56586a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213065" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$120\\cdot 140\\text{ mod }9=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & ≡13\\cdot 9+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡13\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 140 & ≡15\\cdot 9+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡15\\cdot 0+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\cdot 140 & ≡3\\cdot 5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡15\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡9+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n120\\cdot 140\\text{ mod }9=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee53d91-2594-53d2-9d42-3fe1afc3ede2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ee53d91-2594-53d2-9d42-3fe1afc3ede2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413480} :answer-field/id #uuid "88403233-5d65-5d7f-a884-198469bdac12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e40b6e95-46c9-501b-9cf3-a3ca43d4de46" :question/math-academy-id "q-213066" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$94\\cdot 74\\text{ mod }8=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}94 & ≡11\\cdot 8+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 74 & ≡9\\cdot 8+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}94\\cdot 74 & ≡6\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n94\\cdot 74\\text{ mod }8=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ecf2566-0fbb-5c3c-bd2b-a10c6c13ce83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ecf2566-0fbb-5c3c-bd2b-a10c6c13ce83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413494} :answer-field/id #uuid "52eefa79-7a00-5342-a326-3242d4e53917" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3bd2c71-c0a1-5bcf-99be-f98d89cf7b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-213071" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n${8}^{6}\\text{ mod }9=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}={8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2} & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 9+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{6} & ≡{8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}\\,\\,\\text{mod}\\,9=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cf77a2-4eb2-52c0-9c0d-e1645c260e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413466} {:answer/id #uuid "0d761cac-98d1-57a4-961b-d01d89bb8dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413467} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fd01be-dff1-54f7-8430-1207d43687c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413516} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe624ca-e41b-54cb-83a5-2c4bec9e21b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413531} {:answer/id #uuid "f7893b80-75f3-5c3b-9f72-1ca9d9f6c35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbe624ca-e41b-54cb-83a5-2c4bec9e21b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413531} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6e82e0-cc26-55f9-90de-b2b59a403f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73829fe7-6343-5529-8a3e-7f6982fe6b1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213082" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${19}^{19}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{19}^{19}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & ≡10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{19}^{2} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${19}^{19}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{19}^{19}={({19}^{2})}^{9}\\cdot 19\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{19}^{19} & ≡{({19}^{2})}^{9}\\cdot 19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{9}\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{19}^{19}\\text{ mod }10=9\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${19}^{19}$ is $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eac1284-7e8a-5cd8-9b01-790d6397d4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413471} {:answer/id #uuid "a606d71f-4abf-56b6-b723-489fde7cf778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413514} {:answer/id #uuid "b65606a0-5323-5b38-88b9-565e502ff7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65606a0-5323-5b38-88b9-565e502ff7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413519} :answer-field/id #uuid "427f6584-c0af-52e4-81d5-6532a479ad5b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413551} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b8aea0-1d18-58fa-bfdd-4c182e291f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413470} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd1c6c-0479-553c-a784-a40c9ffd8e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413493} {:answer/id #uuid "b32bab38-80c0-58ee-aa2a-f14d8389a1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b32bab38-80c0-58ee-aa2a-f14d8389a1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413517} :answer-field/id #uuid "4db3cfdb-b728-56df-9b15-86079fd51bef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eca33d65-a659-5a03-8cda-7de1fade1db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-213124" :question/problem "Given that $u≡v\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nSince the modulus is unchanged in both statements, let's multiply by $5$ and apply the first property.\n\n$$\nu≡v\\,⟹\\,5u≡5v\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a1677a-5c04-5a45-a564-8c69e8a4b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413472} {:answer/id #uuid "7f141946-1c43-5094-b193-1606569a4874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413504} {:answer/id #uuid "82e0f3af-6b97-591d-9ecb-884c1849cb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413505} {:answer/id #uuid "cb288acd-51c3-5990-bbed-76cf3f8b1472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413527} {:answer/id #uuid "f047ef3d-27cd-555d-bd60-7dcb9abcab96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20a1677a-5c04-5a45-a564-8c69e8a4b6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413472} :answer-field/id #uuid "04c27edf-55f3-5d46-8580-2a951a40bef4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "99665861-b940-5f61-80d5-9c97648d3f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213133" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $2x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)?$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $15$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $2,$ and $8\\cdot 2=16$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 8\\cdot 2x & ≡8\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 16x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+15x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+0x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff8646e-75ee-56e3-9225-450328cee04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413468} {:answer/id #uuid "22d2c81b-0b19-5496-9c41-4e747d1da9df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413473} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9cb5e3-85f6-561e-9093-c94e6d9abf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413496} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d94439-6cba-5374-b5a5-caa715e4b5b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413533} {:answer/id #uuid "e435e15c-46e8-54cf-b47a-2035d47492ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22d2c81b-0b19-5496-9c41-4e747d1da9df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413473} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7775cb9-ebf4-544c-952d-3118f995104e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40d1d6db-2cfa-5253-ab0a-58b0f4105c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-213139" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $7x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)?$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $7,$ and $3\\cdot 7=21$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 3\\cdot 7x & ≡3\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 21x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+20x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+0x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e76e936-9f5c-5e18-b336-ecbbbe4f66fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413479} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7543a4-44f3-591c-a04d-6ced229012de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413498} {:answer/id #uuid "a0741894-a46c-5df4-aa88-7332950a6864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413513} {:answer/id #uuid "f3112c2f-7669-53d5-b05b-66668ce08852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413540} {:answer/id #uuid "f7302721-0826-5833-9781-3d754e8258c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e76e936-9f5c-5e18-b336-ecbbbe4f66fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413479} :answer-field/id #uuid "b24580b0-549e-52ef-8518-91948f4e984f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f6da8c7-058a-5fb2-9ce1-1161fee7e94e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213171" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${28}^{18}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{28}^{18}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & ≡20+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{2} & ≡{8}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {28}^{6} & ≡{({28}^{2})}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{4}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${28}^{18}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{28}^{18}={({28}^{6})}^{3}\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{18} & ≡{({28}^{6})}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(4)^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{28}^{18}\\text{ mod }10=4\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${28}^{18}$ is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ee589d-41dd-514c-9baf-76daf28eb959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413512} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dabb8c-6524-5da7-a90b-c3cd126d21e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413535} {:answer/id #uuid "eb74c9c6-a30e-5a54-8b11-c5961ee030c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8dabb8c-6524-5da7-a90b-c3cd126d21e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413535} :answer-field/id #uuid "90300d85-4a51-50a2-a299-6e653a1cb36c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a68e88c-54bf-5c63-ba79-072dec97003c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413487} {:answer/id #uuid "5705870f-d933-531e-a16a-43e901ed4939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413492} {:answer/id #uuid "87839af9-ade8-5f64-a086-270fb3250d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a68e88c-54bf-5c63-ba79-072dec97003c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413487} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b3178a-a2ad-5e3b-87c5-69822d78d4d6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429c0671-53eb-580a-bb20-0838f8c82926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413481} {:answer/id #uuid "44171d2c-8cd4-581d-9539-b26372a15528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413483} {:answer/id #uuid "72bf9960-d3d8-5c97-b6ad-3321009b5c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44171d2c-8cd4-581d-9539-b26372a15528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413483} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccdccd76-2c21-545a-987d-d111b3a32d9e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11d5fb97-c633-54cb-abf0-947c2b0b0a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-213175" :question/problem "Given that $w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's multiply our congruence by $2$ and apply the second property.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\, & ⟹\\,2\\cdot w≡2\\cdot z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 8) \\\\ w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\, & ⟹\\,2w≡2z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b80a56e-c829-5e05-9c76-7a0b2e001220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413477} {:answer/id #uuid "b82a41c9-3f94-5a68-a88b-e972b19da354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413520} {:answer/id #uuid "b910e2e2-943c-5104-b1ad-704aa8711025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413522} {:answer/id #uuid "c283465c-37c3-5dcb-9033-b8f74eb40b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413523} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8c4405-00c9-59d2-ba23-c4c7852c42d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b910e2e2-943c-5104-b1ad-704aa8711025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413522} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c4a31d-bd95-5f8c-8294-722cc10f2925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "838b9afb-8969-5aff-9073-fc6f44eddea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213181" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${13}^{13}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{13}^{13}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & ≡10+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{13}^{4} & ≡{3}^{4}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${13}^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{13}^{13}={({13}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 13\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{13}^{13} & ≡{({13}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{3}\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{13}^{13}\\text{ mod }10=3\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${13}^{13}$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc7c2b3-26fe-52ba-840f-cc9a5abd5758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413478} {:answer/id #uuid "435c4276-bb89-5e54-9226-0a5f36cba6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413482} {:answer/id #uuid "48c7861e-daa2-5f32-8cbe-7e879c2a04bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413486} {:answer/id #uuid "85f30528-69a2-5386-9ced-f8a2a9aa251c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413506} {:answer/id #uuid "d68d2076-28d4-5eef-999b-f6df1b2a030f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f30528-69a2-5386-9ced-f8a2a9aa251c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413506} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2c66a9-3896-5725-a5ff-7e8f8b45bda5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42a09307-b06f-57dd-a795-e7f9c01844a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-213184" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${12}^{17}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{12}^{17}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & ≡10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{4} & ≡{2}^{4}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {12}^{8} & ≡{6}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡36\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${12}^{17}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{12}^{17}={({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{17} & ≡{({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(6)^{2}\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡72\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{17}\\text{ mod }10=2\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${12}^{17}$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1354f5fe-aec3-598e-a11c-ecb2baf266d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413469} {:answer/id #uuid "3114b013-db2a-5732-82d6-7346aac261fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413475} {:answer/id #uuid "8e99904f-599e-570f-9b78-5cc24914671a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413509} {:answer/id #uuid "c358ed21-b0f9-58ea-96b0-9537635fff34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413525} {:answer/id #uuid "f7facb81-4920-5297-86bc-cd1ce30a844e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e99904f-599e-570f-9b78-5cc24914671a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413509} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74f7ec1-3d25-58dd-8633-311ea5f4ae4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b06970b0-8be9-550a-81a5-76772304b000" :question/math-academy-id "q-213186" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${29}^{13}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{29}^{13}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & ≡20+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{29}^{2} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${29}^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{29}^{13}={({29}^{2})}^{6}\\cdot 29\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{29}^{13} & ≡{({29}^{2})}^{6}\\cdot 29\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{6}\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{29}^{13}\\text{ mod }10=9\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${29}^{13}$ is $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55107169-1347-5d61-9f4e-046e76adf857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55107169-1347-5d61-9f4e-046e76adf857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413491} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a4a822a-0df9-511d-8cdb-21b5b97be2e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f365d70-97c4-5d2a-be4d-f7de85215156" :question/math-academy-id "q-213204" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$83\\cdot 96\\text{ mod }10=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}83 & ≡8\\cdot 10+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 96 & ≡9\\cdot 10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}83\\cdot 96 & ≡3\\cdot 6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡10+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n83\\cdot 96\\text{ mod }10=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4728c8e-2946-54fe-b1c8-574c9966d95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4728c8e-2946-54fe-b1c8-574c9966d95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413518} :answer-field/id #uuid "158bdf28-7538-5205-9155-7eb2e0864758" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a7e58ae-7402-5947-902d-187649afe03f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213206" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$113\\cdot 119\\text{ mod }10=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113 & ≡11\\cdot 10+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 119 & ≡11\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113\\cdot 119 & ≡3\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡27\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n113\\cdot 119\\text{ mod }10=7.\n$$"}]}
